From 87e06c0ea453fe6950c97a8144306efea3a4db45 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: milliluk-erik <build@localhost>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 12:41:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 27/44] gdb: register/GIME/flat-RAM debug queries + cycles

The qxroar. namespace carries no raw chip registers. XRoar's own
-gdb-pseudo-regs already exposes those as real registers in the target
description -- SAM and both PIAs on a Dragon, the GIME and both PIAs on a
CoCo 3 -- where GDB can name them and "info registers" shows them. An
earlier version of this patch had a qxroar.sam get/set doing the same job
through a vendor query, which is strictly worse: no name, no type, no XML,
and a second way to spell a thing that already had one. It is gone.

What is left here is what a register dump cannot express: the GIME's
logical-to-physical translations, flat physical memory that bypasses the
MMU map entirely, and the input side channels.
---
 src/gdb.c   | 371 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 src/xroar.c |   6 +
 2 files changed, 351 insertions(+), 26 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/gdb.c b/src/gdb.c
index 209fd2e..faad5ce 100644
--- a/src/gdb.c
+++ b/src/gdb.c
@@ -30,10 +30,55 @@
  *
  * Breakpoints and watchpoints are supported ('Z' and 'z').
  *
+ * This namespace deliberately holds NO raw chip registers. XRoar's own
+ * -gdb-pseudo-regs exposes those properly, as registers in the target
+ * description -- SAM and both PIAs on a Dragon, the GIME and both PIAs on a
+ * CoCo 3 -- so GDB can name them and 'info registers' shows them. There was a
+ * qxroar.sam here doing the same job worse; it is gone. What belongs here is
+ * what a register dump cannot express: the MMU translations below, flat
+ * physical memory, and the input side channels.
+ *
  * Some standard, and some vendor-specific general queries are supported:
 
- *      qSupported      | XX... | report PacketSize
- *      qAttached       | 1     | always report attached
+ *      qxroar.trace-pc | XXXX    | get PC of current instruction -> 4 hex digits
+ *      qSupported      | XX...   | report PacketSize
+ *      qAttached       | 1       | always report attached
+
+ * GIME-machine-only (coco3) debug registers, alongside the standard PC/D/X/Y
+ * etc above. All read-only, all live state computed at query time unless
+ * noted:
+
+ *      qxroar.task     | XX      | active MMU task, 0 or 1 (live $FF91 TR bit)
+ *      qxroar.block    | XX      | 8K physical RAM block mapped at live $pc
+ *      qxroar.paddr    | XXXXX   | phys addr of the GIME's last bus cycle (gimep->Z, a snapshot, not recomputed)
+ *      qxroar.xpc      | XXXXX   | phys addr the live $pc maps to right now (computed fresh, not a snapshot)
+ *      qxroar.physmem:ADDR,LEN | hex bytes | read LEN bytes of flat physical
+ *                        RAM at ADDR (same addressing as -load-ram's addr=).
+ *                        LEN caps at 512: the reply buffer holds 512 hex
+ *                        pairs. Bigger reads answer E00; chunk them.
+ *                        Bypasses the CPU/MMU map entirely -- works on a
+ *                        bank that isn't currently windowed into any logical
+ *                        address. ADDR/LEN are hex, no 0x prefix.
+
+ * Only these vendor-specific general sets are supported:
+
+ *      Qxroar.physmem:ADDR,LEN:DATA | write hex-pair DATA (LEN bytes) to flat
+ *                                     physical RAM at ADDR. Same addressing/
+ *                                     bypass as the read side.
+ *      Qxroar.axis:A,V            | set synthetic-joystick axis A (0=X,1=Y) to
+ *                                     raw hex V (0..ffff, centre 8000)
+ *      Qxroar.button:N,S          | press (S!=0) / release (S=0) synth button N
+ *      Qxroar.stopin:N            | next continue stops after N more cycles
+ *                                     (hex), wherever PC is, unless a
+ *                                     breakpoint stops it first
+ *      Qxroar.key:SCAN,S          | press (S!=0)/release (S=0) raw matrix key
+ *                                     by DSCAN scancode (PIA path)
+ *      Qxroar.type:HEX            | queue hex-encoded text to auto_kbd typer
+
+ * The four derived values above are as far as this should go. Anything that is
+ * simply a chip register -- SAM, PIA, the GIME's own $FF9x -- belongs in
+ * -gdb-pseudo-regs, which already has the machine interface this TODO used to
+ * ask for. Add it there, not here.
 
  */
 
